# Environment variables
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.template
# Committed on purpose: Vite VITE_* vars are inlined into the public browser
# bundle, so they are never secret. Committing this means a plain `git pull` +
# deploy points the frontend at the right API with no dashboard step.
!.env.production

# Vercel
.vercel

# AI & Agent artifacts
.gemini
.agent
~
*.tmp
_shot.html
scratch/

# Local DB dumps — never commit tenant data
backups/

# Per-environment config. NOT committed.
#
# .env                 shared secrets (db, jwt, vapid, r2, email)
# .env.production      production-only values
# .env.development     development-only values
#
# .env.production used to be committed "because VITE_* values are public
# anyway". They are — but committing it means the repository decides which API
# a build talks to, which is the one thing that has to be set deliberately per
# deployment. Vercel's own environment variables are the place for it.
.env
.env.production
.env.development
.env.*.local

.vercel
# NOTE: `vercel link` also appended `.env*` here. Removed deliberately — the
# rules above already ignore every real env file while making exceptions for
# .env.example and .env.template, which are committed on purpose, and a blanket
# pattern this far down the file silently overrides both.
